+## Single images
+
+Tiles and 9-slice frames must each be their own image, because `ScaleType` `Tile` and `Slice` work
+on the whole image and not on a sprite rect.
+
+For 9-slice images, `SliceScale = 0.5` because the art is exported at 2x. The corners then draw at
+their 1080p size, and the UIScale grows them back to full resolution on a 4K screen.
+
+### B1 Panel / Ornate: 9-slice (full color)
+
+Note: *"Dialog background. Stretches to fit its content, from 240 x 160 up to 960 x 640. Corner flourishes must not stretch."*
+
+- **Figma prep:** make a copy of the panel at **112 × 112** (1x). Put the four 48 × 48 corner
+  flourishes (`40:84`–`40:87`) in the four corners, keep the 4 px gold inside stroke along the
+  edges and the dark fill in the middle, and delete the Note. Each edge's middle 16 px must hold
+  only plain stroke and fill, since that part stretches.
+- **Export:** **224 × 224 px** (2x).
+- **ImageLabel:**
+
+| Property | Value |
+|---|---|
+| `ScaleType` | `Slice` |
+| `SliceCenter` | `Rect.new(96, 96, 128, 128)` |
+| `SliceScale` | `0.5` |
+| `Size` | the dialog's size, anywhere from 240 × 160 to 960 × 640 |
+| `ImageColor3` | `(255, 255, 255)` |
+| `BackgroundTransparency` | `1` |
+
+Its smallest size (240 × 160) is larger than two corners together (96 × 96), so the corners never overlap.
+
+### B2 Slot / Empty: 9-slice (white)
+
+Note: *"Empty inventory slot. Slot size is a player setting from 80 to 128."* Roblox can't draw a
+dashed stroke natively, so it is baked.
+
+- **Figma prep:** a copy of the slot at its design size **96 × 96** (1x), corner radius 12, with the
+  2 px inside dashed stroke (dash 8, gap 6) set to **white at 100%** opacity. No fill.
+- **Export:** **192 × 192 px** (2x).
+- **ImageLabel:**
+
+| Property | Value |
+|---|---|
+| `ScaleType` | `Slice` |
+| `SliceCenter` | `Rect.new(32, 32, 160, 160)` (16 px corners at 1x: 12 radius + 2 stroke + 2 margin) |
+| `SliceScale` | `0.5` |
+| `Size` | the slot-size setting: 80 × 80 to 128 × 128 |
+| `ImageColor3` | `(255, 255, 255)` |
+| `ImageTransparency` | `0.7` (the design's 30% white stroke) |
+| `BackgroundTransparency` | `1` |
+
+Known trade-off: the dashes on the edges stretch with the slot. At 80 px they are about 0.75× their
+design length, and at 128 px about 1.5×. The corners and the stroke width stay exact. Check that all
+four corners look the same once exported, since Figma starts the dash pattern at one corner. If they
+don't match, redraw the edges so every corner starts on a dash.
+
+### B3 Card / Ribbon: 9-slice shape (white) + native label
+
+The ribbon has different radii per corner: 16 at top-left and bottom-right, 0 at top-right and
+bottom-left (`rectangleCornerRadii [16,0,16,0]`). `UICorner` can only round all four corners the same,
+so the shape is baked in white and tinted. The "NEW" label stays a native TextLabel so it can be
+translated and changed.
+
+- **Figma prep:** a **48 × 48** (1x) rectangle with radii TL 16 / TR 0 / BR 16 / BL 0, filled **white**. No text.
+- **Export:** **96 × 96 px** (2x).
+- **ImageLabel:**
+
+| Property | Value |
+|---|---|
+| `ScaleType` | `Slice` |
+| `SliceCenter` | `Rect.new(32, 32, 64, 64)` |
+| `SliceScale` | `0.5` |
+| `Size` | `UDim2.fromOffset(240, 56)` as designed. Can grow with longer translated text |
+| `ImageColor3` | `(222, 67, 82)` |
+| `BackgroundTransparency` | `1` |
+
+- **Label (native TextLabel, child of the ImageLabel):** text "NEW", Builder Sans **ExtraBold**,
+  size 22, line height 28 (`LineHeight ≈ 1.27`), `TextColor3 (255,255,255)`, centered both ways.
+
+### B4 Background / Dot Grid: seamless tile (white)
+
+Note: *"The dot grid repeats behind every full-screen menu, at any screen size."* The sample is 32 separate
+dots at 5% opacity. Drawing that natively would take hundreds of frames, but one tile does the job.
+
+- **Figma prep:** a **24 × 24** (1x) frame with one **white** 2 × 2 dot at x 11, y 11 (the sample's
+  dots repeat every 24 px, starting 11 px in). Opacity 100%, transparent background.
+- **Export:** **48 × 48 px** (2x), with the dot at 4 × 4 px at (22, 22).
+- **ImageLabel:**
+
+| Property | Value |
+|---|---|
+| `ScaleType` | `Tile` |
+| `TileSize` | `UDim2.fromOffset(24, 24)`, in offsets so the dots keep their spacing at any screen size |
+| `Size` | `UDim2.fromScale(1, 1)` behind the menu |
+| `ImageColor3` | `(255, 255, 255)` |
+| `ImageTransparency` | `0.95` (the frame's 5% opacity) |
+| `BackgroundTransparency` | `1` |
+
+### B5 Badge / Legendary (full color)
+
+Roblox has no diamond gradient, so the badge is baked: the 64 × 64 circle with the diamond gradient
+(`#FFD94D` → `#D9590D`) and the white star.
+
+- **Export:** the whole badge frame `40:145`, **128 × 128 px** (2x), transparent outside the circle.
+- **ImageLabel:** `Size = UDim2.fromOffset(64, 64)`, `ScaleType = Fit`, `ImageColor3 = (255,255,255)`, `BackgroundTransparency = 1`.
+- If more rarity badges get designed later, put them all on one sheet together (cell 136, art 128) instead of making one image each.
+
+### B6 Logo (full color)
+
+The wordmark has a 6 px outside stroke and the group has an orange glow (drop shadow, blur 24, no
+offset). A glow that follows a shape is baked into the image.
+
+- **Figma prep:** put the Logo group centered in a transparent **768 × 288** (1x) frame. The logo
+  with its stroke and glow takes about 700 × 254, so the glow fits inside without being clipped.
+- **Export:** **1024 × 384 px**: the frame at **1.333x** (in Figma's export settings, use `1024w`).
+  A full 2x export would be 1536 × 576, over the 1024 limit.
+- **ImageLabel:** `Size = UDim2.fromOffset(768, 288)`, `ScaleType = Fit`, `ImageColor3 = (255,255,255)`, `BackgroundTransparency = 1`.
+- Trade-off: this stays sharp up to a UIScale of 1.33 (1440p screens) and gets a little soft on 4K.
+  If that matters, export it as two halves instead. Each half is a 384 × 288 (1x) slice exported at
+  2x to **768 × 576 px**, drawn as two ImageLabels side by side. Check the seam where they meet at 1x and 2x.
+
+### B7 Glow (white)
+
+A soft ellipse with a layer blur. Roblox can't blur a single frame, so it is baked.
+
+- **Figma prep:** the 120 × 120 ellipse with its 30 px layer blur, filled **white at 100%**,
+  centered in a transparent **192 × 192** (1x) frame so the blur isn't clipped.
+- **Export:** **384 × 384 px** (2x).
+- **ImageLabel:** `Size = UDim2.fromOffset(192, 192)` centered where the ellipse's center goes,
+  `ScaleType = Fit`, `ImageColor3 = (255, 200, 60)`, `ImageTransparency = 0.4` (the design's 60% fill),
+  `BackgroundTransparency = 1`.
+
+### B8 Item Portraits: one image per item (full color)
+
+Note: *"120 item portraits in total, and more are added every update. The item detail pane shows one at a time at 128 x 128."*
+These are shown one at a time, out of a large set that keeps growing, so each portrait is its own
+image. A sheet would load 119 portraits nobody is looking at, and would have to be uploaded again
+every update.
+
+- **Export:** each portrait **256 × 256 px** (2x), **square with no rounded corners**. The 12 px
+  rounding is done natively. Name the files after the layers (`Item Portrait/Torch` →
+  `ItemPortrait_Torch.png`).
+- Make sure each portrait's source image is at least 256 px. Figma will upscale a smaller source
+  without warning, and the result will be blurry.
+- **ImageLabel:** `Size = UDim2.fromOffset(128, 128)`, `ScaleType = Crop` (matches Figma's FILL),
+  `BackgroundTransparency = 1`, with a child `UICorner` `CornerRadius = UDim.new(0, 12)`.
+
